    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4010",
      "problem": "What is the amplitude of $f(x)=2\\cos{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255908/assets/5310c3b337a5b508cf70c0e71ca4f4467270db038185f9cc71f9597d0bb2697d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y_\\max=2$ and the minimum value is $y_\\min=-2.$ Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Amplitude } & =\\frac{{y}_{max}-{y}_{min}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
